WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:10.086
This is Rutt Etra Web, a browser recreation of the 1973 Rutt Etra scan processor, an analog video synthesizer.

2
00:00:10.541 --> 00:00:12.315
No camera needed for this tour.

3
00:00:12.315 --> 00:00:16.322
Click Pattern, and the built-in pattern generator becomes the picture.

4
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.588
The image is drawn as horizontal scan lines.

5
00:00:19.588 --> 00:00:24.502
Brighter areas push the lines toward you, so the picture becomes a landscape.

6
00:00:24.953 --> 00:00:28.494
Drag in the render window to orbit the virtual camera around it.

7
00:00:30.137 --> 00:00:33.636
The Controls panel on the left holds every parameter.

8
00:00:33.636 --> 00:00:35.947
Type in the search box to find one.

9
00:00:35.947 --> 00:00:38.456
Here, Z Push, the depth of the raster.

10
00:00:38.909 --> 00:00:40.730
Drag a knob up to turn it up.

11
00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:42.301
More push, deeper relief.

12
00:00:43.419 --> 00:00:48.684
The Patch Bay on the right is the same instrument, laid out as a hardware faceplate.

13
00:00:48.684 --> 00:00:50.565
Maximize it to see every jack.

14
00:00:51.016 --> 00:00:53.416
Right-click empty space to add a node.

15
00:00:53.416 --> 00:00:55.816
We will add an LFO, a slow oscillator.

16
00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:59.892
Drag from the LFO's output jack onto Z Push's input.

17
00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:01.495
The cable is the patch.

18
00:01:01.952 --> 00:01:05.045
Restore the window, and the raster now breathes on its own.

19
00:01:05.504 --> 00:01:08.518
Pick a source, shape it, and patch movement into it.

20
00:01:08.518 --> 00:01:11.242
Next, we will learn our way around the windows.
